===Etymology===
From {{bor|en|fr|antonyme}} (1840s and 1850s), which was modeled on earlier {{m|fr|synonyme}} and influenced by the [[etymon#Noun|etymons]] of {{der|en|grc|ἀντωνυμία||pronoun}}; credit for popularization of the French loanword's [[naturalize#English:_linguistics|naturalization]] into English is given principally to Charles John Smith and his 1867 book ''Synonyms and Antonyms: Or, Kindred Words and Their Opposites. Collected and Contrasted.''<ref name="Smith-1867">{{cite-book |en |author=Charles John Smith |origyear=1867 |year=1868 |title=Synonyms and Antonyms: Or, Kindred Words and Their Opposites. Collected and Contrasted |edition=2nd |publisher=Bell and Daldy |location=London |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=plQIAQAAIAAJ&printsec=frontcover&source=gbs_ge_summary_r&cad=0#v=onepage&q&f=false }}</ref> {{surf|en|ant-|-onym}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{enPR|ǎnt′ənĭm'}}
* {{IPA|en|/ˈæn.təˌnɪm/}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-antonym.ogg|a=GA}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɪm|s=3}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|semantics}} A word which has the [[opposite]] meaning of another word.
#: {{syn|en|counterterm|opposite}}
#: {{ant|en|synonym}}
#: {{cot|en
|coordinate term|cohyponym|;<!--fellow type of the same larger class, but not diametrically opposite-->
|antiphrasis|;<!--intentionally discrepant meaning-->
|near-synonym|parasynonym|plesionym|;<!--nearly the same-->
}}
#: {{ux|en|“rich” is an '''antonym''' of “poor”; “full” is an '''antonym''' of “empty”}}
## A word that describes one end of a scale, while its opposite describes the other end, such as ''large'' versus ''small''; a {{l|en|gradable antonym}}.
##* {{quote-book|en|year=2005|author=Andrew John Merrison; Aileen Bloomer; Patrick Griffiths; Christopher J. Hall|title=Introducing Language in Use|url=http://www.worldcat.org/title/introducing-language-in-use-a-coursebook/oclc/705997377|page=111|text=All four lines of the pattern are required to establish that ''hot'' and ''cold'' are '''antonyms'''. <br/>''The water is hot'' entails ''The water is not cold''. <br/>''The water is cold'' entails ''The water is not hot''.<br/>''The water is not hot'' does not entail ''The water is cold''. <br/>''The water is not cold'' does not entail ''The water is hot''.}}

